{"toolCallId":"call_d622ba8f905c4d57b26988914c09751a","title":"Wrote ./specs/zz-bench-authoring/001-recommend-level-quiet-flag/spec.md","kind":"edit","content":[{"type":"diff","path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.worktrees/224-deepseek-final2/specs/zz-bench-authoring/001-recommend-level-quiet-flag/spec.md","oldText":"---\ntitle: \"Feature Specification: Add a quiet flag to recommend-level.sh\"\ndescription: \"[YOUR_VALUE_HERE: What is broken, missing, or inefficient? 2-3 sentences describing the specific pain point.]\"\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"recommend level quiet flag\"\n  - \"add a quiet flag to recommend level sh\"\nimportance_tier: \"normal\"\ncontextType: \"general\"\n---\n<!-- SPECKIT_TEMPLATE_SOURCE: spec-core + level2-verify + level3-arch | v2.3 -->\n# Feature Specification: Add a quiet flag to recommend-level.sh\n\n<!-- SPECKIT_LEVEL: 3 -->\n\n\n---\n\n## EXECUTIVE SUMMARY\n<!-- FILL: Write three sentences: the problem, the change and the proof. GOOD: Callers parse a 17-line report to read one value. The flag prints one line. A line count proves it. WEAK: This document describes the feature. -->\n\n[2-3 sentence high-level overview for stakeholders who need quick context]\n\n**Key Decisions**: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Major decision 1], [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Major decision 2]\n\n**Critical Dependencies**: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Blocking dependency]\n\n---\n<!-- ANCHOR:metadata -->\n## 1. METADATA\n<!-- FILL: Fill every row: level, priority, status, date and branch. Use the real branch name. GOOD: Priority P1. Status Draft. Branch `feat/recommend-level-quiet`. WEAK: Priority TBD. Status maybe. -->\n\n| Field | Value |\n|-------|-------|\n| **Level** | 3 |\n| **Priority** | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: P0/P1/P2] |\n| **Status** | Draft |\n| **Created** | 2026-10-25 |\n| **Branch** | `scaffold/001-recommend-level-quiet-flag` |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:metadata -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:problem -->\n## 2. PROBLEM & PURPOSE\n<!-- FILL: State the problem in two sentences: what breaks, and for whom. GOOD: The recommend-level script prints a 17-line report, so a caller that needs only the level cannot parse the answer. WEAK: The script has issues with output. -->\n\n### Problem Statement\n[YOUR_VALUE_HERE: What is broken, missing, or inefficient? 2-3 sentences describing the specific pain point.]\n\n### Purpose\n[YOUR_VALUE_HERE: One-sentence outcome statement. What does success look like?]\n<!-- /ANCHOR:problem -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:scope -->\n## 3. SCOPE\n<!-- FILL: List three to five things this change does, each starting with a verb. GOOD: Add a `--quiet` flag that prints only the recommended-level line. WEAK: Update the script and docs. -->\n\n### In Scope\n- [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Deliverable 1]\n- [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Deliverable 2]\n- [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Deliverable 3]\n\n### Out of Scope\n- [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Excluded item 1] - [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: why]\n- [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Excluded item 2] - [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: why]\n\n### Files to Change\n\n| File Path | Change Type | Description |\n|-----------|-------------|-------------|\n| [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: path/to/file.js] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Modify/Create/Delete] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Brief description] |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:scope -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:requirements -->\n## 4. REQUIREMENTS\n<!-- FILL: Write one testable requirement per row. Name the exact command or output. GOOD: With `--quiet`, stdout holds exactly one line that starts with `Recommended Level:`. WEAK: The flag should work well. -->\n\n### P0 - Blockers (MUST complete)\n\n| ID | Requirement |\n|----|-------------|\n| REQ-001 | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Requirement description] |\n\n### P1 - Required (complete OR user-approved deferral)\n\n| ID | Requirement |\n|----|-------------|\n| REQ-002 | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Requirement description] |\n\n> Acceptance criteria for these requirements live in `acceptance-criteria.md`,\n> which is the document that decides whether this packet may close.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:requirements -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:success-criteria -->\n## 5. SUCCESS CRITERIA\n<!-- FILL: Write one measurable outcome per line. A command can prove it. GOOD: `recommend-level.sh --quiet --loc 500 --files 15` prints one line and exits 0. WEAK: The feature is successful. -->\n\n- **SC-001**: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Primary measurable outcome]\n- **SC-002**: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Secondary measurable outcome]\n<!-- /ANCHOR:success-criteria -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:risks -->\n## 6. RISKS & DEPENDENCIES\n<!-- FILL: Name one dependency and one risk. Give each a concrete mitigation. GOOD: A caller parses the level line and breaks when the label changes. Mitigation: keep the label stable and test it. WEAK: There may be some risk. -->\n\n| Type | Item | Impact | Mitigation |\n|------|------|--------|------------|\n| Dependency | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: System/API] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: What if blocked] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Fallback plan] |\n| Risk | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Risk description] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: High/Med/Low] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Mitigation strategy] |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:risks -->\n\n---\n\n\n## 7. NON-FUNCTIONAL REQUIREMENTS\n<!-- FILL: Name one measurable limit per line. Write `N/A` and one reason when the packet runs no service, or delete the whole section. GOOD: The flag adds no measurable time: the same 40 ms run. WEAK: Performance is good. -->\n\n### Performance\n- **NFR-P01**: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Response time target - e.g., <200ms p95]\n\n### Security\n- **NFR-S01**: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Auth requirement - e.g., JWT tokens required]\n\n### Reliability\n- **NFR-R01**: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Uptime target - e.g., 99.9%]\n\n---\n\n## 8. EDGE CASES\n<!-- FILL: Name the empty input, the maximum input and the failure path. One line each. GOOD: Empty input: the script exits 2 with usage. Maximum: 10,000 files scores in under one second. WEAK: Handle edge cases. -->\n\n### Data Boundaries\n- Empty input: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: How system handles]\n- Maximum length: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Limit and behavior]\n\n### Error Scenarios\n- External service failure: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Fallback behavior]\n- Network timeout: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Retry strategy]\n\n### State Transitions\n- Partial completion: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Recovery behavior]\n- Session expiry: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: User experience]\n\n---\n\n## 9. COMPLEXITY ASSESSMENT\n<!-- FILL: Score each row with the number the change earns. The total must match the level. GOOD: Scope 4/25: one file and 12 lines. Total 18/100. WEAK: Complexity is medium. -->\n\n| Dimension | Score | Triggers |\n|-----------|-------|----------|\n| Scope | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: /25] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Files: X, LOC: Y, Systems: Z] |\n| Risk | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: /25] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Auth: Y/N, API: Y/N, Breaking: Y/N] |\n| Research | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: /20] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Investigation needs] |\n| Multi-Agent | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: /15] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Workstreams: X] |\n| Coordination | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: /15] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Dependencies: X] |\n| **Total** | **[YOUR_VALUE_HERE: /100]** | **Level 3** |\n\n---\n\n## 10. RISK MATRIX\n<!-- FILL: List each risk with impact, likelihood and one mitigation. GOOD: R1: a caller parses the label. Impact M, likelihood L. Mitigation: a test pins the label. WEAK: Some risks exist. -->\n\n| Risk ID | Description | Impact | Likelihood | Mitigation |\n|---------|-------------|--------|------------|------------|\n| R-001 | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Risk] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: H/M/L] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: H/M/L] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Strategy] |\n\n---\n\n## 11. USER STORIES\n<!-- FILL: Write one story per user. Name the actor, the want and the benefit. GOOD: As a build script, I want one parseable line, so that I can skip the report. WEAK: Users want a quiet mode. -->\n\n### US-001: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Title] (Priority: P0)\n\n**As a** [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: user type], **I want** [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: needed behavior], **so that** [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: benefit].\n\n**Acceptance criteria:** see `acceptance-criteria.md` (rows referencing this story).\n\n---\n\n### US-002: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Title] (Priority: P1)\n\n**As a** [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: user type], **I want** [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: needed behavior], **so that** [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: benefit].\n\n**Acceptance criteria:** see `acceptance-criteria.md` (rows referencing this story).\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:questions -->\n## 12. OPEN QUESTIONS\n<!-- FILL: Write each open question as one line. Write `None` when nothing blocks. GOOD: Does `--quiet` suppress warnings? No, stderr is unchanged. WEAK: Edge cases are unclear. -->\n\n- [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Question 1 requiring clarification]\n- [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Question 2 requiring clarification]\n<!-- /ANCHOR:questions -->\n\n---\n\n## RELATED DOCUMENTS\n<!-- FILL: Leave the four links in place. -->\n\n- **Implementation Plan**: See `plan.md`\n- **Task Breakdown**: See `tasks.md`\n- **Verification Checklist**: See `tasks.md`\n- **Decision Records**: See `decision-record.md`\n\n---\n\n\n","newText":"---\ntitle: \"Feature Specification: Add a quiet flag to recommend-level.sh\"\ndescription: \"Callers that need only the recommended level must read a 16- or 17-line report or depend on jq to parse JSON. A --quiet flag prints the single Recommended Level line and leaves every existing output path unchanged.\"\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"recommend level quiet flag\"\n  - \"add a quiet flag to recommend level sh\"\nimportance_tier: \"normal\"\ncontextType: \"general\"\n---\n<!-- SPECKIT_TEMPLATE_SOURCE: spec-core + level2-verify + level3-arch | v2.3 -->\n# Feature Specification: Add a quiet flag to recommend-level.sh\n\n<!-- SPECKIT_LEVEL: 3 -->\n\n\n---\n\n## EXECUTIVE SUMMARY\n\nA caller that needs one value from `recommend-level.sh` must currently read a multi-line report or parse JSON with `jq`. This packet adds a `--quiet` flag that prints only the `Recommended Level:` line and leaves the default text and JSON outputs untouched. A line count and a byte comparison against the report's own level line prove the contract.\n\n**Key Decisions**: `--quiet` is an output-only switch evaluated after scoring, so no scoring path changes; `--json` keeps precedence when both flags are passed.\n\n**Critical Dependencies**: The existing `Recommended Level: <N> (<Name>)` label, which downstream callers already parse.\n\n---\n<!-- ANCHOR:metadata -->\n## 1. METADATA\n\n| Field | Value |\n|-------|-------|\n| **Level** | 3 |\n| **Priority** | P2 |\n| **Status** | Draft |\n| **Created** | 2026-10-25 |\n| **Branch** | `scaffold/001-recommend-level-quiet-flag` |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:metadata -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:problem -->\n## 2. PROBLEM & PURPOSE\n\n### Problem Statement\n\n`recommend-level.sh` prints a report whose first line is the recommendation and whose remaining lines are supporting detail. Measured on the current script, `--loc 500 --files 15` prints 16 lines and the full-risk variant prints 17, while `--no-recommend-phases` prints 12. A caller that needs only the level has two poor options: parse a human report whose layout may change, or request `--json` and depend on `jq`, which a minimal CI image may not ship.\n\n### Purpose\n\nLet a shell caller read the recommended level from a single line, while the default and JSON outputs stay byte-identical for every existing consumer.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:problem -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:scope -->\n## 3. SCOPE\n\n### In Scope\n- Add a `--quiet` option to the argument parser and to the output dispatch.\n- Print exactly one line under `--quiet`: `Recommended Level: <N> (<Name>)`.\n- Feed that line and the report's level line from one shared formatter so the two cannot drift.\n- Document the flag in the file header, the `--help` options table and one example.\n- Keep stderr, exit codes and every existing output path unchanged.\n\n### Out of Scope\n- A short `-q` alias - not requested; the option surface grows by exactly one long flag.\n- Any change to the report layout or the label text - existing parsers depend on both.\n- A new one-line structured mode - `--json` already covers structured output.\n- A new test harness - two existing Node test scripts already exercise the script with `--json`.\n\n### Files to Change\n\n| File Path | Change Type | Description |\n|-----------|-------------|-------------|\n| `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/recommend-level.sh` | Modify | Header options, help text, `QUIET` global, parser case, shared level-line formatter, quiet output branch |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:scope -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:requirements -->\n## 4. REQUIREMENTS\n\n### P0 - Blockers (MUST complete)\n\n| ID | Requirement |\n|----|-------------|\n| REQ-001 | `recommend-level.sh --quiet` is accepted by the option parser and exits 0 for valid numeric input. |\n| REQ-002 | With `--quiet`, stdout is exactly one line matching `Recommended Level: <0-3> (<name>)`, with no leading blank line. |\n| REQ-003 | The quiet line is byte-identical to the `Recommended Level:` line the default text report prints for the same inputs. |\n| REQ-004 | Without `--quiet`, the default text output is unchanged line for line, including blank lines. |\n| REQ-005 | `--quiet` changes no computed value: level, total score, confidence and phase fields are identical to the same invocation without the flag. |\n| REQ-006 | `--json` output is unchanged; when `--json` and `--quiet` are both passed, JSON is printed and `--quiet` has no effect. |\n| REQ-008 | Error handling is unchanged: invalid or missing input still writes to stderr, exits 1, and writes nothing to stdout under `--quiet`. |\n\n### P1 - Required (complete OR user-approved deferral)\n\n| ID | Requirement |\n|----|-------------|\n| REQ-007 | `--help` lists `--quiet` in its options table and shows one example invocation. |\n| REQ-009 | `--quiet` composes with `--no-recommend-phases`, `--phase-threshold` and the risk flags without changing the one-line contract. |\n\n> Acceptance criteria for these requirements live in `acceptance-criteria.md`,\n> which is the document that decides whether this packet may close.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:requirements -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:success-criteria -->\n## 5. SUCCESS CRITERIA\n\n- **SC-001**: **Given** valid numeric input, **When** the script runs with `--quiet`, **Then** `wc -l` prints 1 and the exit code is 0.\n- **SC-002**: **Given** the same input, **When** the report's level line and the quiet output are compared, **Then** `diff` prints nothing.\n- **SC-003**: **Given** `--loc 500 --files 15`, **When** the script runs without `--quiet`, **Then** stdout is 16 lines and matches the pre-change capture.\n- **SC-004**: **Given** `--json --quiet`, **When** the script runs, **Then** stdout is the 36-line JSON document and `recommended_level` is present.\n- **SC-005**: **Given** no `--loc` and no `--files`, **When** the script runs with `--quiet`, **Then** stdout is empty, stderr carries the error and the exit code is 1.\n- **SC-006**: **Given** `--quiet --no-recommend-phases`, **When** the script runs, **Then** stdout is exactly the one level line.\n- **SC-007**: **Given** the `--help` output, **When** it is read, **Then** it contains a `--quiet` row and one quiet example.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:success-criteria -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:risks -->\n## 6. RISKS & DEPENDENCIES\n\n| Type | Item | Impact | Mitigation |\n|------|------|--------|------------|\n| Dependency | Bash 3.2+ features already used by the script (`[[ ]]`, arrays, `local`) | Script cannot run | No new shell feature is introduced; the existing interpreter floor is unchanged |\n| Dependency | The `Recommended Level:` label that callers parse | A label change breaks them | The label is untouched, and one shared formatter keeps a single copy of it |\n| Risk | The quiet branch copies the report's leading blank line | `wc -l` returns 2 and the one-line contract fails | AC-002 asserts exactly one line; AC-003 compares bytes |\n| Risk | A later edit changes one output path and not the other | Silent drift between report and quiet line | `format_level_line()` feeds both paths (ADR-001) |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:risks -->\n\n---\n\n\n## 7. NON-FUNCTIONAL REQUIREMENTS\n\n### Performance\n- **NFR-P01**: `--quiet` adds one string comparison in the parser and one branch at output; it starts no subprocess and reads no file, so runtime stays within measurement noise of the current run.\n\n### Security\n- **NFR-S01**: The script reads no credentials and writes no files; `--quiet` adds only a boolean flag to the existing input surface.\n\n### Reliability\n- **NFR-R01**: The exit-code contract is unchanged: 0 on success and 1 on user error, with `set -euo pipefail` still in force.\n\n---\n\n## 8. EDGE CASES\n\n### Data Boundaries\n- Empty input: `--quiet` with neither `--loc` nor `--files` writes the existing error to stderr, exits 1 and writes nothing to stdout.\n- Maximum length: `--loc 100000 --files 5000` clamps each score at its cap and still prints exactly one line.\n\n### Error Scenarios\n- External service failure: N/A - the script makes no network or service call.\n- Network timeout: N/A - same reason.\n\n### State Transitions\n- Partial completion: N/A - one stateless run either prints the line and exits 0 or writes to stderr and exits 1.\n- Session expiry: N/A - the script holds no session or persistent state.\n\n---\n\n## 9. COMPLEXITY ASSESSMENT\n\n| Dimension | Score | Triggers |\n|-----------|-------|----------|\n| Scope | 4/25 | Files: 1, LOC: ~20, Systems: 1 |\n| Risk | 3/25 | Auth: N, API: N, Breaking: N (default and JSON output preserved) |\n| Research | 3/20 | Read one output function, one help block and two existing test scripts |\n| Multi-Agent | 2/15 | Workstreams: 1 |\n| Coordination | 2/15 | Dependencies: 0 |\n| **Total** | **14/100** | **Level 3 (scaffolded)** |\n\nThe declared level is a scaffolding choice, not a complexity result. The change scores 14/100 on this table; the packet is Level 3 because `create.sh` was invoked with `--level 3`.\n\n---\n\n## 10. RISK MATRIX\n\n| Risk ID | Description | Impact | Likelihood | Mitigation |\n|---------|-------------|--------|------------|------------|\n| R-001 | The quiet line drifts from the report line | M | L | One `format_level_line()` helper feeds both paths; AC-003 pins byte equality |\n| R-002 | The quiet branch leaves a blank line or extra output | M | M | AC-002 asserts exactly one line and AC-003 compares bytes |\n| R-003 | A caller passing `--json --quiet` gets an unexpected shape | L | M | ADR-002 records the precedence and AC-004 pins it |\n\n---\n\n## 11. USER STORIES\n\n### US-001: One parseable line for scripts (Priority: P0)\n\n**As a** CI or build script that only needs the level, **I want** the recommendation on a single stdout line, **so that** I can read it without parsing a report or depending on `jq`.\n\n**Acceptance criteria:** see `acceptance-criteria.md` (rows referencing this story).\n\n---\n\n### US-002: The report stays the default (Priority: P1)\n\n**As a** developer running the script by hand, **I want** the full report to remain the default, **so that** adding the flag never changes what I already see.\n\n**Acceptance criteria:** see `acceptance-criteria.md` (rows referencing this story).\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:questions -->\n## 12. OPEN QUESTIONS\n\n- None. The only judgment call, `--json` precedence over `--quiet`, is recorded as ADR-002 in `plan.md`.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:questions -->\n\n---\n\n## RELATED DOCUMENTS\n\n- **Implementation Plan**: See `plan.md`\n- **Task Breakdown**: See `tasks.md`\n- **Verification Checklist**: See `tasks.md`\n- **Decision Records**: See ADR-001 and ADR-002 in `plan.md`\n\n---\n\n","_meta":{"cognition.ai/linesAdded":65,"cognition.ai/linesRemoved":57}}],"rawInput":{"file_path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.worktrees/224-deepseek-final2/specs/zz-bench-authoring/001-recommend-level-quiet-flag/spec.md"},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/inferenceToolName":"write"}}
